stix1.1.5 Linux raw source obtains getsid result then passes it to Pid::from_raw_unchecked; that constructor asserts raw>0.\"], \"file\": \"crates/bd-cache-sync/tests/common/session_fixture.rs\", \"id\": \"REQ-QA-004\", \"inference_and_limits\": \"Successful getsid returning zero follows from the syscall result conversion and assertion stack. Linux kernel processes without user session membership are the likely triggering class, but the failing PID/raw SID was not logged; that specific identity is unproven. No Linux reproduction or new Cargo execution performed by this reviewer.\", \"issue\": \"The Linux hosted lifecycle test aborts because the all-host-process census calls rustix::process::getsid on unrelated processes; rustix 1.1.5 Linux raw backend assumes successful SID values are strictly positive. Hosted stack shows from_raw_unchecked assertion raw > 0 fails, then Drop repeats the same inventory call and panics during cleanup, causing non-unwinding SIGABRT.\", \"line\": 174, \"required_consumer_correction\": \"Correct inventory in the test helper so unrelated Linux processes with zero or otherwise non-user session identifiers are safely excluded before use of a typed positive-PID API. For example, collect PID/SID/PGID through the owned bounded inventory command and parse/validate raw identifiers, filtering to the known positive fixture SID; retain safe identity checks for kill targets. Preserve existing lifetime bounds, real PythonJob paths and cleanup ownership, and avoid repeating a panicking census in Drop. Combine this with the FTQ010 requirement to deadline-bound, cancel and reap the inventory subprocess itself. Rerun native lifecycle tests and required Linux/macOS/Windows hosted checks at corrected exact HEAD.\", \"routing\": \"Include this concrete Linux portability correction in existing REQ-QA-004 developer acceptance work and related FTQ010 helper repair. No new canonical ID required because REQ-QA-004 already requires every current-head CI job green